    guideExtra:"Start by studying the meadow before you draw. Pip is safest when the barrier creates a short route that bees cannot simply walk around, but a long wall is expensive and gives the swarm more surface to push. Leave a little breathing room around the puppy so a repair does not trap your next stroke. When a barrier flashes red, repair that weak section first instead of replacing the whole shape. The first valid line starts the wave, so you have time to plan while the meadow is quiet. Once bees are moving, watch their approach rather than the line you are drawing: a safe-looking wall can still leave an open corner behind Pip. Two separated terrain anchors are stronger than one loop. A single stone lets a wall pivot or peel; wrapping the same stroke around two distinct stones, corners, or approved terrain points makes the wall hold and glow gold. If bees push a loose wall, do not chase the wall with frantic redraws. Wait for the gap they are trying to open, then add a short hook that closes that route. Nectar is limited, and efficient repairs usually earn more stars than a second large circle. Before each later chapter, reread the chapter hint: new hives, split approaches, gusts, fragile gaps, and mixed waves each change what the shortest safe line should look like. After a clear, the next route unlocks automatically and your best stars remain in this browser. A failed attempt costs no account data; simply return to the stage list, study the red opening, and try a different shape. You can also replay an earlier route to practice efficient shapes, compare the stars earned from short repairs, and learn which side of the meadow gives the bees the fewest choices. The stage summary records your progress, while the guide remains available whenever you need to refresh the basic rules.",
